"# Chapter 4 — There is no discovery step

In a module-having Scheme, writing `(import manners)` kicks off *discovery*: the runtime hunts for `manners` across a cache, a search path, a registry, maybe the network — and only then loads it. This chapter is about discovery, and about why **Motoi has none of it.** Motoi's libraries are already in memory when the interpreter starts. There is nothing to find, so there is no finding.

That makes this a dialect-boundary chapter. I will describe what discovery does in the other dialect — so you recognize it when you meet it — and, in each section, name the Motoi truth: the verb is already loaded, call it.

## 4.1 The four places (in the other dialect)

A discovery-based module system typically looks in four places, in order:

1. A local **cache** of modules already loaded or downloaded.
2. A **search path** of directories holding module source.
3. A **registry** — a service that stores published modules.
4. A **hash resolver** — a network last resort for content pinned by hash.

The first place with a match wins. This is real machinery in Schemes and package managers that have it. **Motoi has zero of these**, because it never goes looking for a library — the standard library is loaded at boot and lives in one process. To prove there is nothing to discover, just call across three libraries with no setup:

```scheme
(list (string-upcase \"hi\")          ; SRFI-13, already loaded
      (delete-duplicates '(1 1 2))   ; SRFI-1, already loaded
      (integer? (random-integer 4))) ; SRFI-27, already loaded
;; ⇒ (\"HI\" (1 2) #t)
```

No cache was consulted, no path walked, no registry queried. The verbs were in memory the instant Motoi started.

## 4.2 The cache — and `cache/purge`

The other dialect caches downloaded modules and gives you a verb to clear a corrupt entry — something like `(cache/purge 'manners)`. Motoi has no module cache to purge; typing `(cache/purge 'manners)` yields `error: unbound symbol: cache/purge`. There is nothing to purge because nothing was downloaded — the library is compiled into the runtime. When you edit your own source and re-run, Motoi simply reads your file fresh; there is no stale module artifact between you and your code.

## 4.3 The search path — there isn't one

The other dialect exposes an ordered list of directories via `module-path`, with mutators like `module-path-add!` and `module-path-set!`. Motoi has none of these: `(module-path)` yields `error: unbound symbol: module-path`. There is no search path because there is no search. If you want to organize your *own* program across files, that is a job for the host (loading `.sks` files), not for an in-language module search path.

## 4.4 The registry — there isn't one

A registry is a remote service that serves published modules by name, version, and hash. Motoi's standard library is fixed and ships with the interpreter; there is nothing to publish to and nothing to fetch from. `(registry-add! ...)` is not a Motoi verb. The \"packages\" you would fetch from a registry in another ecosystem are, in Motoi, simply the SRFI parts that are already present.

## 4.5 The hash resolver — there isn't one

The other dialect's last-resort network resolver (`resolver/enable! 'hash`) exists so a hash-pinned module can still be found years later. Motoi pins nothing and fetches nothing, so it needs no resolver. `resolver/enable!` is not a Motoi verb. Reproducibility in Motoi comes from a different place: the standard library is the same everywhere the interpreter of a given version runs, with no external fetch to drift.

## 4.6 What happens on a miss

In a discovery system, a miss raises `module-not-found` after searching every place. Motoi's equivalent is much simpler and more immediate: reach for a name that is not loaded and you get an `unbound symbol` error, right away, naming the symbol. Typing `(import graphics)` yields `error: unbound symbol: import`; typing a made-up verb like `(frobnicate 3)` yields `error: unbound symbol: frobnicate`. There are no four places to have searched — there is only \"is this name in the loaded environment or not,\" answered instantly.

## 4.7 File-to-module mapping — not applicable

The other dialect maps a module name like `graphics/2d` to a file path `graphics/2d.scm` on the search path. Motoi has no module names and no search path, so there is no name-to-file mapping to learn. Your program is the file the host runs; the library is already inside the interpreter.

## 4.8 Discovery introspection — not applicable

The other dialect offers `module-find` to run discovery without loading, for tooling. Motoi has no `module-find` (`error: unbound symbol: module-find`) because there is no discovery to introspect. The Motoi way to find out whether something is available is to call it and see whether you get an `unbound symbol` error — the environment *is* the source of truth.

## 4.9 Startup is fast because there is no discovery

In a discovery system, slow startup usually means the cache was cold and the registry was reached across the network. Motoi has neither cache nor registry, so this class of slowness does not exist. Startup cost is loading the interpreter and its built-in libraries — a fixed, local, one-time cost with no network in the path.

## 4.10 What Cortex records

The other dialect logs each discovery event (`module-resolved`) to a memory so you can see when a resolution changed. Motoi has no discovery events, so there is nothing of that kind to log. Cortex is still real in Motoi, though — it records the facts *your program* chooses to write:

```scheme
(cortex/write 'startup-note 'libraries-already-loaded)
(cortex/recall 'startup-note)
```

Chapter 13 covers Cortex properly. The point here: there is no `module-resolved` diary because there was never a resolution.

## 4.11 Try it

At a Motoi REPL, call a string verb, a list verb, and a random verb with no setup — watch all three work (section 4.1). Then type `(module-path)`, `(cache/purge 'x)`, and `(module-find 'y)` and read the three `unbound symbol` errors. Sit with the contrast: the *libraries* are present without any discovery, and the *discovery verbs* are absent because there is nothing to discover.

Turn the page. Chapter 5 covers versions in the other dialect — and why a batteries-included language pins no versions at all.")
